worker: clone executables on macOS instead of hardlinking them

Applies to NativeLink v1.8.6 (37f3b206a8e6d55613abae2b18a0665fff24e096).
build-worker.sh applies it; ../../README.md, "Version and patch", says why
the macOS workers cannot run an upstream release without it.

dyld resolves @loader_path and @executable_path from the main image's path
as the kernel reports it. For a hardlinked file that can be any name of the
inode, and an executable staged out of the worker's flat, content-addressed
executable store has a name there as well as in the action tree. Anything
carrying LC_RPATH = @loader_path/../lib and loading @rpath/... then searches
next to the store instead of next to itself. rustc is the common case:

  dyld: Library not loaded: @rpath/librustc_driver-<hash>.dylib
    Referenced from: .../worker-content.exec/d/<digest>-412504
    Reason: tried: '.../worker-content.exec/d/../lib/librustc_driver-...'
    (no such file)

clonefile(2) gives the destination its own inode with copy-on-write blocks:
one name, the action tree path, at roughly hardlink cost and with no extra
disk usage. It copies the source's modes verbatim, so the executable
variant's read-only 0o555 survives. Only executables take the new path, and
only on macOS; every other file stays a hardlink out of the shared store.
A failed clone (another volume, a filesystem without clones) falls back to
the hardlink, which is the behaviour without this patch.

This is brianduff/nativelink 3125a4fd (one commit on v1.6.6) carried
forward. What changed on the way to v1.8.6:

- v1.8.6 repairs a link whose source the store evicted while the tree was
  being staged. A clone whose source is gone is therefore reported as
  NotFound, the way the hardlink reports it, instead of being retried as a
  hardlink, so it reaches that repair; and the repair of an executable
  clones as well (link_executable). Before, a repaired executable would
  have been a hardlink again.
- PendingLink already has an `executable` field upstream, so the patch no
  longer adds one.
- New tests at the worker level: a staged executable has its own inode and
  one link while a plain input is still a hardlink, and a repaired
  executable has one link.
- BUILD.bazel gives the binary a version. A Bazel build otherwise reports
  0.0.0, and the suffix lets `nativelink --version` tell a worker binary
  that carries this patch from an upstream release.

Not upstream: TraceMachina/nativelink#3137 is open, as are the two pull
requests that address it another way (#3138, #3191). Drop this file when a
release stages macOS executables on their own inode.

